\title{The best session cap is set by the processor cache, not by the step count: 1 MiB}
\taxon{Insight}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{driver}
\tag{performance}
\meta{origin}{rlvm-machine/SWITCHES.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Source: [design-switches-document](design-switches-document), section "Sessions"; finding F24 of [rep-machine-M6](rep-machine-M6); [rep-P8](rep-P8), section 4.5; [rep-P9](rep-P9), section 4.5; [the session on wave 6 and the pipeline](ses-CVTE). A warm session keeps the tables of the machine across accepted declarations until a cap on their size.}
\ul{
\li{The step count falls as the cap grows, because more memo entries are found. On a Mathlib slice: 31.7 M steps at 1 MiB, 26.9 M at 4 MiB, 24.6 M at 16 MiB, 23.0 M at 64 MiB, 22.7 M at 1 GiB.}
\li{CPU time and wall clock rise as the cap grows, because the tables leave the cache. With the former cap of the driver, 16 MiB, a warm session was slower than no warm session: Init with one worker 9.5 s against 8.23 s for the plain machine and 7.70 s at 1 MiB; native image in the arena container on a loaded host, two rounds.}
\li{With the default switch set, native image, 8 workers, quiet windows: 512 KiB and 1 MiB are within 2 percent of each other on every corpus; 2 MiB costs 3 to 4 percent of CPU on Init, Std, cedar and Mathlib; 4 MiB costs 4 to 7 percent and 17 percent of wall clock on cedar.}
\li{The question whether 17 percent more steps on a Mathlib slice at 1 MiB against 4 MiB should change the default was answered by wall clock on all of Mathlib with 8 workers: 29.3 s per warm pass at 1 MiB against 30.6 and 30.3 s at 2 and 4 MiB. The cap stays.}
\li{The cap is a property of the tables of the machine, so the machine has a cap of its own beside the memory account of the driver. A reset of the machine alone is always legal.}
}
\transclude{ex-UT0I}
\p{For comparison, nano-lean drops a session when its arena passes 64 MiB and gruszka truncates its arrays at 8 MiB; see [study-nano-lean](study-nano-lean) and [study-gruszka-pestka](study-gruszka-pestka). This is one of the cases of [counts do not stand for time](ins-KFM6). What a warm session saves in steps at each cap, against one declaration per session, is in [the region question](ins-KMXU).}
\p{See also: [warm sessions and the session cap](design-warm-sessions); [the decision for 1 MiB](dec-KXE1).}
